To make my comment clear...
There are people in r.g.r.n that would rather see a generator with
"real-Sokoban-difficulty".  I understand that wish but don't think
that it's feasible.  I'd look for a non-trivial suboptimal solution.
Your map quality would meet (at least :-) my requirements.

> It's certainly a different kind of complexity than in the real Sokoban 
> maps.  Unlike those, the hardest parts in my maps often come from a 
> small cluster of 3 or 4 boulders (this might not always be obvious if 
> you just look at the maps).

I've noticed some trivial clusters and a few clusters where you have
to think a bit about.  That's a good start.  Ideally the ratio should
move towards the harder clusters, but the existing possibilities for
optimization depend of course on the used algorithm.

>  > When you decide to publish
>  > your algorithms we could better discuss about possible improvements.
> Yeah, I will do that.  One thing that could certainly be improved is the 
> definition of "difficulty".  How does one define how difficult a certain 
> map is?  Perhaps you have some idea of how to do this?

Hmm, that's a hard question.  One quick shot for a metric would be:
Count how many of the choices for possible moves would lead to deadlocks,
and at which level that deadlocks occur (by level I mean the depth of the
path in a search tree of positions that might be handled by a backtracking
algorithm).  But, honestly, that must be well thought of...

> And then there is of course the pure computational optimization.  The 
> maps I posted took 16 hours on average on an Athlon 750MHz with 512MB 
> RAM.  If you need four maps (one for each level), that's 64 hours.

Oops.  Now *that's* a real problem.  To be part of the map generation
within NH this must rather be of *sub-second* magnitude than hours/days.

Hmm, using other methods to incorporate the maps also drop out; like e.g.
load them from an external server through internet would break locality
principle; build them at compile time would also be no choice, because
the compilation of the *complete* NH code needs just a single minute.
A possible choice might be to precompile a small database of your maps,
and dynamically load these as are the quest levels today.  Since the
maps do not require much memory space that may be a feasable way.

